            <ArticleTitle>Continuous coronary perfusion in redo aortic valve replacement following prior coronary surgery; an old trick for new dogs?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>437-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1510/icvts.2009.218503</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The optimal myocardial protection method for aortic valve replacement in the setting of prior coronary artery bypass surgery remains a subject of debate. Protection is particularly challenging when a patent pedicled internal thoracic artery graft supplies a proximally obstructed left anterior descending artery. Herein, we describe a modification of an old technique; continuous coronary perfusion, which can be used in selected, anatomically suitable cases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
